Package 5, the Vim keys: what is not built yet, and what waits for whom.

The rest of FEATURES.md section 2 is ported from Neovim 0.12.6 and checked
against nvim --headless (tools/conform.py, tests/conform-recorded.json).
Every line below is a known difference, and those conform.py's random
sequences reach have a rule in tests/conform-known.json (on the keys and
text, with an example that must still differ).  Measured 2026-11-18, seed 1,
2,000 sequences: with package 4's core (PR 20) 0 differ outside the rules
(3 inside them); with ref/core.c 38 differ, from what it stubs.

Not built (FEATURES.md section 2), part two of package 5:
- search (search.c, Neovim's search.c): / ? n N * # g* g# with their
  offsets (e s b and a line), as motions, after operators, in Visual mode
  and with . to repeat; 'ignorecase', 'smartcase' and 'wrapscan' on.
  tests/test_keys_ex.c checks them against nvim on random keys (cursor,
  error, text); measured 2026-11-19, seed 1, 3,000 searches: 0 differ.
  Not built yet: ";" between searches (/x/;/y), 'incsearch', the search
  history, Visual-mode * and #, register "/.
  'hlsearch' (keys_hl(), next_search_hl() from the window's top line),
  :noh[lsearch] and CTRL-L (Neovim's default map, Normal mode): the same
  test compares the cells 'hlsearch' changes on Neovim's screen (80
  columns, the lines the window shows) after every random case.
  The pattern engine (regex.c, Neovim's regexp.c): magic levels, classes,
  collections, multis, groups and back references, \zs \ze, \& and the
  look-arounds, 'ignorecase' and \c \C.  tests/test_keys_regex.c checks it
  against substitute() in nvim on random patterns and lines; measured
  2026-11-19, 16 seeds of 10,000: 4 differ, all in the oddity below.  Not
  built yet: \%[ \%V \%# \%l \%c \%v and the marks, \%#= (the engine
  choice), \z( (syntax only), a look-behind that reaches into lines above,
  and multi-line text in the random test (\n and \_x are built; :s checks
  them on a buffer).  Ours backtracks, as Neovim's backtracking engine,
  with its stack on the heap: a pattern that backtracks without end on a
  line, such as \(a*\)*b, \(a\|b\)*c on a very long one, gives E363 after
  20 million steps, where Neovim's default NFA engine says E486 at once.
- operators = ! and gc (comment); the surround, align and other section 3
  extras.
- gq's 'comments': Neovim formats with 'formatoptions' tcqj, so a comment
  leader ("# ", "// ", "> ", "- " with 'comments' at their default, a
  Markdown list with its ftplugin's) is kept on each broken line and dropped
  from joined ones; ours treats it as text, as J does (conform.py sends no
  leaders).  Also not built: a cindent buffer's paragraph indent, 'textwidth'
  set (ours is Neovim's 0: the window's width less one, at most 79), gw.
- gq leaves other windows' cursors and the marks past a break on the first
  line; Neovim's open_line() moves them to the new line (OPENLINE_MARKFIX's
  mark_col_adjust()).  core_mark_join() would mark the next edit a join, so
  this waits for a core call from package 4 (reviews/pr-67.md, finding B).
  The same holds for the '> mark of a Visual gq, so gv after it differs
  (H2).
- tag objects it at.
- Insert mode's CTRL-E and CTRL-Y (the character below or above).
- after a t or f that fails, Neovim drops a following & (Neovim's map to
  :&&) typed ahead, where : or g& still run (measured, nvim --headless
  feedkeys, 1 run each; not understood yet); ours runs it.
- ex commands beyond :w :w! :q :q! :wq :x, :Format, :KittenPaw, ranges, :<line> and :s: :g,
  :norm, :d, :y, :e, :sp, :vs, :b; "|" between commands; the ":" motion
  (d:5, which beeps). Visual @ and Q need :norm.
- :s (sub.c, Neovim's do_sub()): :s/pat/rep/ with the flags & c g e n r
  i I, a count, :& :&& :~, the keys & (Neovim's map to :&&) and g&; in the
  replacement & \0-\9 ~ \u \l \U \L \e \E \r \n \t; matches over
  line breaks.  tests/test_keys_ex.c checks it against nvim on random
  command lines (cursor, error, text); measured 2026-11-19, seeds 1-9,
  3,000 each: 0 differ.  The c flag's question (y n a q l CTRL-E CTRL-Y,
  the line shown with its substitutions so far, the match in IncSearch) is
  checked on random answers; a question still open at the end is not
  compared, since Neovim's feedkeys() waits for its key.  Not built yet:
  \= (an expression), 'inccommand' (the preview), the p # l flags'
  printing, a backwards range (ours E493; Neovim asks to swap), the names
  :sg :si :sI :sr :sc (s with flags and no blank).
- ranges (checked by tests/test_keys_ex.c against nvim on random command
  lines): a range before :w :q :wq :x gives E481, where Neovim writes part
  of the buffer (E140 without !) or closes window N; a line number past
  LONG_MAX / 4 saturates, where Neovim's wraps; a ":" typed after "'<,'>"
  (blanks between) is left out, since Neovim takes it differently from one
  after "1,2" (measured, 3 runs, not understood yet).
- Insert CTRL-K digraphs, gi.
- marks and the jump list: m ' ` g' g` with a-z ' ` " ^ [ ] < > { } ( ),
  as motions and in ex ranges (one getmark(), as mark_get_local()), CTRL-O
  and CTRL-I (Tab) through the jump list, and '' set where Neovim calls
  setpcmark() (G gg N% % H M L * # / ? n N ( ) { } [( ]) a( i( ' ` and the
  first match of :s; not :N).  '[ and '] are the buffer's (opmarks()).
  tests/test_keys_ex.c checks them against nvim on random keys; measured
  2026-11-21, seeds 1-3, 3,000 each: 0 differ.  Waiting for the core
  (package 4, asked): '[ '] after a file is read, '. and the change list
  (g; g,), A-Z and 0-9 (set, they stay unset: E20), the jump of an undo,
  '' moved by J and the saved '' (w_prev_pcmark) moved by edits, and a
  jump list entry in another buffer (CTRL-O beeps there).
- folds, spell, snippet jumps, CTRL-S: with their packages.
- U (undo line) is not in FEATURES.md, so not built; Visual gUU reaches it
  (gU on the selection, then U).
- CTRL-W Tab (CTRL-W CTRL-I) needs windows; a C whose motion
  fails sends the insert's C-w and Tab to Normal mode.

Waits for package 4's core (src/core, PR 20), passing with it (measured):
- CTRL-D, CTRL-U, CTRL-E, CTRL-Y, CTRL-F, CTRL-B, zz zt zb H M L, and gj
  gk with 'wrap': ref/core.c stubs them (they match with package 4's core
  and Neovim at scrolloff=6; its tests/test_core_screens.c types them).
- gv after edits: its ends are the buffer's '< and '> marks, which only
  package 4's core moves.
- columns of grapheme clusters: ref/core.c's core_vcol and core_col_at step
  by code point.
Waits for package 4, still differing with PR 20's core:
- a group the keys open without an edit (d{ that cannot move) saves no
  marks, so after v Esc d{ v j Esc u on "bar\nab,", gv covers lines 1-2
  where Neovim's u_undoredo restores '> to line 1 (reviews/pr-52.md,
  finding 4; needs a core.h change from thread 0).
- core_vcol at a byte inside a cluster (x + U+0301 with the cursor after x)
  gives the cluster's end; Neovim's getvcol gives its start. Typing before
  a lone mark makes such a cursor ("x\n\u0301", j i a Up C-w: Neovim's
  column 0, ours 1); CTRL-O and Del there differ the same way
  ("\u0301 \"a b\"", I é C-o).
- I Down from a blank a combining mark is on, after the cursor came there
  with k ("    \u0301 -3\n).\n    'c' 7", j dd k I Down): Neovim's column
  2, ours 3; not traced yet (owner package 5, with package 4's core_vcol
  inside a cluster above).

Not traced to a fix yet (owner package 5):
- gg, then C-v < on a line that is only a Tab ("\t\n\tfoo", gg C-v <):
  Neovim shifts it to 4 spaces, ours to 7; without gg Neovim gives 7 too.
- a counted insert, Left, then Tab ("\tFoo", 2 a Left Tab Esc): Neovim
  inserts one space, ours four; like s Tab below, Neovim's softtabstop
  counts from a stale cached w_virtcol.
- after A x Esc, v 3B y, then a linewise yank (y 2j or y ][), Neovim's
  wanted column is 5 cells and ours 0 ("\tab\n    cd", A b x Esc v 3 B y
  y 2 j j: Neovim's column 5, ours 0).
- block $ I with CTRL-W and CTRL-R over a ZWJ emoji that r and s split
  earlier: Neovim and ours write different split characters (the case is
  the example in tests/conform-known.json; no shorter one found).
- s on a Tab that ends its line, then Tab ("(\t", l s Tab): Neovim's cached
  w_virtcol stays the Tab's last cell, since del_bytes() backs the cursor up
  and op_change() steps it forward again before check_cursor_moved() looks,
  so softtabstop counts from column 7 (one space); ours counts from 1 (three).
  The core drops its cached column on every edit (changed_common() keeps it
  for an edit after the cursor).

Not yet like Neovim, for the Visual follow-up after PR 67 (with package
4's cases: block l from a Tab, block O past a short line, G and block o
after $ keeping curswant MAXCOL):
- a Visual block p, then C-v X ("    x\u0300\u0301 \"a b\"", I CR ( b Esc
  C-v gg p y i( C-v X): ours deletes nothing and leaves the cursor past
  the line's end (not traced further).
- a block yanked from a Tab, then p ("\t} , -3", C-v Y p): Neovim pads
  the put out to the Tab's last cell ("\t} , -3  "), ours puts it inside
  the Tab.
- $, then C-v on a Tab and Esc ("\t", $ C-v Esc >>): Neovim's curswant is
  the Tab's first cell (column 0 after >>), ours its last (column 7).
- C-v on a Tab, then J ("\t[y] 0x1f }", C-v J a BS): Neovim's cursor
  stays inside the Tab, so a splits it into spaces; ours is on the Tab.
- a block yanked across a Tab, then C-v r ("\t", S 0 BS y Esc C CR Esc
  C-v - y C-v r ,): Neovim replaces at the Tab's first cell, ours after it.

Neovim's own oddities, not copied:
- patterns over a letter with a separate combining accent (e + U+0301):
  Neovim's two engines disagree with each other there, and each with its own
  rules (\(e\|\)\. on it: the NFA engine finds no match, the backtracking
  one matches e and leaves the accent).  Ours follows the NFA engine, which
  Neovim uses unless a pattern needs the other: a class or collection
  matches the letter by its base, a repeated character takes the e and
  leaves the accent, a match never ends before an accent.  The random test
  takes either engine's answer (and none where Neovim loops or gives E363);
  the 4 cases neither gives have a literal e next to the accented letter,
  one listed in the test's KNOWN.
- where Neovim's two engines differ elsewhere, ours gives the backtracking
  one's answer: \@> (:%s/\v @>foo// misses "  foo.bar" in the default
  engine, /z\@>\n\w\{-1,} is E486 there), and a \_[] collection under :s
  with g (:1s/\_[^z]//g on "ab", "cd", "zz" leaves an empty first line in
  the default engine, whose program does not say it can match a line
  break; ours and the other join the lines).  Measured by the review of
  PR 90 against 'regexpengine' 1.
- r, then R and CTRL-U or BS back over the replaced character ("  0", r 3 l R
  C-u): Neovim's replace stack still holds what r replaced and puts it back.
- block I, then Left past the block's start and text ("abcd\nefgh", l C-v j
  I Left x): Neovim puts "a" in the other lines, ours "x" (its
  Insstart_orig stays at the block's start; not traced further).
- block > over blanks that end in a combining mark ("    \u0301", C-v >):
  Neovim drops the mark.
- an operator with v over a sentence that ends the buffer in a multibyte
  character ("a b\nx \u10d0", gU v as; d v as leaves one byte of it):
  Neovim ends the motion inside the character.
- Visual A over a 3-byte character ("∀" vAx), V A over a 2-byte one ("λ"
  V A 𐐀) or a cluster ("x" with U+0300 U+0301, V A .) and C-v $ ~ over a
  4-byte one ("-3 𐐀x"): Neovim writes a split character. Also when the
  character was typed, not in the text ("    ", s 𐐀 Esc v A 0).
- C-v on a Tab then ~ u U over a multibyte character ("\té ab cd", C-v w ~;
  "\t𐐀X", C-v w u):
  Neovim moves the character to the end of the line (" ab cd É").  The Tab can be on another line of the block ("\t0x1f\n  ẞß", C-v ]] U).
- an operator with g_ over trailing blanks after a multibyte character
  ("  ", a ა Esc $ d g_): Neovim's nv_g_underscore_cmd steps back a
  byte at a time and ends inside the character, deleting all but its first
  two bytes.
- Tab typed after two gq on a line indented past 'textwidth' (84 spaces
  and "a", gqgq gqgq i Tab): Neovim's softtabstop counts from the w_virtcol
  that internal_format()'s coladvance() left, as in s Tab; ours keeps that
  stale column for motions (gq gg gql gg) but not for Insert mode.
  The same after a Visual block gq on a blank line ("  ", C-v _ gq r Tab):
  the block's coladvance() to its first column leaves w_virtcol 0.
- CTRL-V U with a code past U+10FFFF ("ab", i C-v U110000 Esc): Neovim
  writes bytes that are not UTF-8 (from 0x80000000 a special-key code); ours
  inserts nothing, and stops adding digits there so the code cannot overflow.

Counts (measured, 2026-11-17): a count folds at 999999999 as Neovim's
does, and the work grows with it.  Large counts are checked by recorded
traces (22202o λ, 12000a, 3000yyp, 22222222x, 9999999999j), not by
fuzz_keys, which keeps counts to one digit to bound its work.  22202o λ
takes 0.30 s here and 0.07 s in Neovim (median of 5 runs each, -O2 driver;
222222o 2.95 s and 0.44 s): 61% of it is the rope's line lookup
(text_line_start, memchr; package 1).  Typing into one long line is
quadratic in both, since each key validates the cursor's column
(insert_check()); 22a CTRL-R over 1,000 recorded "x y BS" takes 4.0 s here
and 1.0 s in Neovim (one run each), most of it in core_vcol (package 4).

Choices:
- go is gg everywhere: Normal, after an operator, Visual (Davy, FEATURES.md).
- Davy's maps (FEATURES.md 4), Normal mode only, as nnoremap: SPC ff
  files, SPC fg grep, SPC fr registers, SPC , buffers, SPC e explorer
  (tools_pick), SPC pv and - oil (tools_oil), SPC g g s l L d b B and
  SPC h s r S u p (git_leader), SPC uk the save kitten on or off
  (kitten_toggle; :KittenPaw plays it); in .org buffers only,
  nvim-orgmode's cit and ciT (org_todo, org_todo_prev: org_todo_step's
  next or previous keyword, one undo step), so there c waits for its next
  key and c then anything else is Vim's c.  They run in main.c's key chain
  (keys_map()), not in keys_feed, so conform.py and the fuzzers, whose
  Neovim has no maps, check the Vim keys alone; test_keys_map checks the
  maps.  Like Neovim's maps they also follow a count or a register, which
  they take, as a <Cmd> map does; a key that completes no map runs Space's
  own motion, then the keys.  A lone SPC waits for the next key (no
  'timeoutlen', as with Davy's which-key); which-key's hint popup is not
  built yet.  Maps that only exist later (SPC i s snippets, SPC u k, SPC x,
  SPC c, SPC d, SPC a, SPC o) join the table with their packages; a map
  typed in a macro is not recorded.
- 'clipboard' is unnamedplus (Davy, 2026-11-18): every yank, delete and
  put of the unnamed register, "+ and "* goes through the system clipboard
  of the Windows or Wayland window (the terminal has none, as Neovim
  without a provider).
- % is Neovim's built-in one for now; Davy wants matchit's word pairs too
  (2026-11-17), a follow-up PR.
- Insert mode CTRL-X (Neovim's completion submode, e.g. CTRL-X CTRL-O)
  is not in FEATURES.md and not built: the CTRL key after it acts alone.
- U (undo line) is not in FEATURES.md and not built.  CTRL-F and CTRL-B
  are not in it either, but package 4's screens test types them.
